          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Score points by winning exactly the number of tricks bid at the start of the round.',
          gameplay: '1. **Bidding**: Each player in turn bids the exact number of tricks they intend to take. 2. **Forced Bid**: The dealer cannot bid an amount that makes the total bids equal to the total available tricks (to ensure someone fails). 3. **Tricks**: Players must follow suit. Highest trump wins; if no trump, highest of led suit wins. 4. **Scoring**: Successfully making the bid earns a base bonus (usually 10 points) plus 1 point per trick taken. Any other result scores zero.',
          keyRules: [
            'Must follow suit if possible.',
            'Must hit the bid EXACTLY; taking more or fewer tricks results in a score of zero for the round.',
            'Trump suit is determined by the card turned up from the stock after the deal.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "In 1-card rounds, an Ace is a guaranteed trick. In higher rounds, bid conservatively—it is usually easier to win an extra trick than to lose one you didn't want.",
          intermediate: 'Counting the Total: Pay attention to the sum of the bids. If the total is much higher than the tricks available, play defensively as players will be fighting over every trick.',
          advanced: "The Dealer's Trap: As the dealer, use your forced-bid constraint to create a 'low-bid' round if you have a weak hand, forcing others to fight for high tricks while you safely duck with your low cards.",
          tips: [
            'Bidding zero is often the hardest and most rewarding strategy.',
            'Track the high trumps—knowing if the King of trumps is still out determines if your Queen is a safe winner.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
